Related reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://dev.to/libme/dependabot-vs-renovate-which-one-stops-the-monday-morning-pr-flood-47ao"&gt;Dependabot vs Renovate: Which One Stops the Monday Morning PR Flood?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://dev.to/libme/fastapi-latency-spikes-under-load-how-one-blocking-call-in-an-async-endpoint-stalls-every-request-29e1"&gt;FastAPI Latency Spikes Under Load: How One Blocking Call in an async Endpoint Stalls Every Request&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://dev.to/libme/cost-attribution-before-cost-optimization-how-to-find-out-what-is-actually-charging-you-5086"&gt;Cost Attribution Before Cost Optimization: How to Find Out What Is Actually Charging You&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I Built an Interactive 3D Anatomy Explorer Using React, TypeScript and Three.js&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've recently completed a project I've been working on called Anatomy Explorer, formerly known as Wildlife Anatomy Museum.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The idea was to build something that makes exploring animal and human anatomy more interactive. Rather than presenting information through ordinary images and text alone, I wanted to create an experience where visitors could explore 3D models, examine different anatomical structures and learn through interaction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What the project offers&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anatomy Explorer brings together different animal species and the human body in one interactive environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some of the main features include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A gallery of different animal and human specimens.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Interactive 3D models that users can rotate and zoom.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Anatomical layers and structures where supported by the available models.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Information panels with details about selected specimens.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A responsive interface for different screen sizes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal was to make the experience useful for anyone interested in anatomy, including students, biology enthusiasts and people who simply want to explore how different organisms are structured.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Technologies I used&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built the application with:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;TypeScript&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Vite&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Three.js&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React Three Fiber&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drei&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tailwind CSS&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Font Awesome&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project is deployed on Netlify.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some thoughts behind the development&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One thing I wanted to achieve with this project was to move beyond building conventional websites and explore how interactive 3D technology could be used in practical web applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Working with React Three Fiber, Three.js and TypeScript gave me an opportunity to explore a different side of frontend development. It also helped me understand how to bring together multiple technologies to create an application that offers more than a static user interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is always room to improve, and I see this project as another important step in developing my skills and becoming better at building useful software.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Explore Anatomy Explorer&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Live Website: &lt;a href="https://wildlife-anatomy-museum.netlify.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://wildlife-anatomy-museum.netlify.app/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd genuinely appreciate feedback from developers, biology students, educators and anyone interested in interactive learning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Feel free to explore the application and share your thoughts. I'd be interested to hear what you think and what you would improve.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My project Vesper originally started from a motion design I found on Dribbble for a smart home integration company.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I really liked the animation and started thinking about how I could turn it into a fully functional website and use it as a personal showcase project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My main focus with Vesper was high-quality design smooth animations and interactive experiences. I built the website using React and GSAP.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One more really cool feature that I implemented is a interactive smart home demo where the visitors can interacts with rooms and change lightings and shading presets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Live website: &lt;a href="https://vesper.aporka.dev/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://vesper.aporka.dev/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Original inspiration: &lt;a href="https://dribbble.com/shots/27231469-Luxury-Smart-Living-Website-Animation-Experience?utm_source=chatgpt.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://dribbble.com/shots/27231469-Luxury-Smart-Living-Website-Animation-Experience?utm_source=chatgpt.com&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Raise your hand if you've ever waited for a full redeploy because another team changed one button in the header. 🙋&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the whole pitch for micro-frontends. Every team ships its own piece of the page whenever it wants, and the page just... picks it up. No coordinated releases. No "can you merge my PR before Friday?".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In Next.js, the go-to tool for this was &lt;code&gt;@module-federation/nextjs-mf&lt;/code&gt;. Then the App Router arrived, and nextjs-mf never followed. So I did what developers do when a tool doesn't exist: I built one. 😅&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's called &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/tariqule/next-federation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;next-federation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, it's open source, and it just hit 0.2. Here's how it works.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


🧩 Two ways to borrow someone else's UI
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quick vocabulary first. The app that &lt;em&gt;provides&lt;/em&gt; the UI is the &lt;strong&gt;remote&lt;/strong&gt;. Your Next.js app that &lt;em&gt;renders&lt;/em&gt; it is the &lt;strong&gt;host&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The remote can hand you UI in two ways.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Islands.&lt;/strong&gt; The remote renders React Server Components on its own server and sends back HTML. Your page embeds it, and it hydrates as its own little React root. The nice part: the remote's code never runs on your server. It keeps its own secrets, its own database and its own problems. 😄&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Remote components.&lt;/strong&gt; The remote ships a client component (Module Federation 2.0 under the hood), and it renders right inside your React tree. It can read your context and use your router, so it behaves like it was always there. Great for things like a cart or a nav menu.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Islands&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Remote components&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Remote's code runs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;On the remote's server&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;In your React tree&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Props&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Plain JSON&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Anything&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Your React context&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Doesn't cross in&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Readable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Good for&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Content the remote owns&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Interactive bits that must feel native&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And yes, you can mix both on the same page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


⚡ Your first island in about five minutes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The remote
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The remote is just a small Vite project. You tell it which islands it serves and who's allowed to embed them:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// vite.config.ts (remote)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nextFederationIslands&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@next-federation/vite'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'vite'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;nextFederationIslands&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'shop'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;base&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://shop.example.com/'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;hosts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://www.example.com'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;islands&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ProductPanel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./src/islands/ProductPanel.tsx'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;An island is an async Server Component. Fetch whatever you need, drop in client components, the usual:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/islands/ProductPanel.tsx (remote)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;getProduct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'../data'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./Counter'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ProductPanel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sku&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sku&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getProduct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sku&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;${product.price}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;section&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


The host
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the Next.js side there are three small pieces. Wrap your config:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// next.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;withNextFederation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@next-federation/next/config'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;withNextFederation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;List the remote and create the island component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// lib/federation.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'server-only'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defineFederation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@next-federation/core'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;createIslands&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@next-federation/next/server'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;federation&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineFederation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;remotes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;shop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;origin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://shop.example.com'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RemoteIsland&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createIslands&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;federation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And from there it's just a component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/page.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RemoteIsland&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'../lib/federation'&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;RemoteIsland&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;remote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"shop"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;island&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ProductPanel"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sku&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'A1'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Loading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;…&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;errorFallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Product&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;unavailable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now when the shop team deploys a new &lt;code&gt;ProductPanel&lt;/code&gt;, your page shows it. You don't rebuild anything. And if their server is having a bad day, your page still returns &lt;code&gt;200&lt;/code&gt; and shows the &lt;code&gt;errorFallback&lt;/code&gt; instead of falling over.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


🗄️ Caching is where I was most stubborn
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Islands go through Next.js's own fetch cache. So a page with a public island can stay completely static, or use ISR, like any other page. Need one island refreshed right now? &lt;code&gt;revalidateIsland()&lt;/code&gt; calls &lt;code&gt;revalidateTag&lt;/code&gt; under the hood. On Next 16 it works with &lt;code&gt;cacheComponents&lt;/code&gt; too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I set myself one rule early and kept it: the library must never quietly turn a static page into a dynamic one. If you've ever lost an afternoon to "why is this page suddenly not cached?!", you know why.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


🔐 Per-user islands, without leaking tokens
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Islands can also be &lt;strong&gt;private&lt;/strong&gt;, meaning they render per signed-in user. After a &lt;a href="https://next-federation-docs.vercel.app/docs/guides/identity" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;short identity setup&lt;/a&gt;, the host mints a short-lived token for the remote, and the remote reads the user with &lt;code&gt;getIdentity()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That token never shows up in your HTML, in Next.js's RSC payload or in browser storage. And I'm not just hoping that's true: the e2e suite checks for it, in &lt;code&gt;next dev&lt;/code&gt; and in production builds.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;RemoteIsland&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;remote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"shop"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;island&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"MyOrders"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Loading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;…&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


🛡️ When does the host trust a remote's code?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the part I thought about the most. If your server renders a remote component, it's running someone else's code on your machine. So the host only does that when the build is &lt;strong&gt;signed&lt;/strong&gt; (or pinned by digest) and every file matches its sha384 hash. Anything else renders in the browser only.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A small confession: while I was preparing 0.2, one test kept failing about half the time. It turned out that, with the right timing, the runtime could load the wrong build of a remote when two builds shared a name. Not fun to track down. But I'd much rather CI finds that than you. 😬&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Remote components can also use &lt;code&gt;next/link&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;next/navigation&lt;/code&gt; or React Router. The build rewires those imports, so the same component navigates through whatever router the host has. App Router, Pages Router, a React Router SPA, it doesn't care.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


🤷 Why only public Next.js APIs?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because I really don't want to rewrite this every time Next.js ships a release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Everything goes through APIs that Next.js documents: &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; with tags, &lt;code&gt;'use cache'&lt;/code&gt;, Route Handlers and &lt;code&gt;unstable_rethrow&lt;/code&gt;. There are no &lt;code&gt;next/dist&lt;/code&gt; imports, and CI fails if one sneaks in. It's a bet that a Next.js upgrade won't break your micro-frontends. Ask me again in a year. 😄&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


👀 Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The live demo is probably the fastest way to get the idea: three hosts (App Router, Pages Router and a React SPA) pulling UI from two separately deployed remotes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Live demo:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://next-federation-docs.vercel.app/docs/live-demo" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://next-federation-docs.vercel.app/docs/live-demo&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Docs:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://next-federation-docs.vercel.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://next-federation-docs.vercel.app&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GitHub:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/tariqule/next-federation" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/tariqule/next-federation&lt;/a&gt; (if you like it, a star really helps ⭐)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's 0.2, so there are rough edges. If you've tried micro-frontends with Next.js before, I'd genuinely like to know what got in your way. Drop it in the comments or open an issue!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;next-federation is an independent open-source project, not affiliated with or endorsed by Vercel.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What carries over
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Vue concept&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;React equivalent&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;How it helps you transition&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Component‑based UI&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Function or class components&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Both frameworks break the UI into reusable, isolated units. Your habit of thinking in “components that own their template and logic” maps directly to React’s JSX‑based components.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Props for data flow&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;props&lt;/code&gt; (read‑only)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;In Vue you pass data with &lt;code&gt;:prop="value"&lt;/code&gt;; in React you use &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;MyComp value={value} /&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. The unidirectional flow is the same mental model.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Local state&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; (hooks) or &lt;code&gt;this.state&lt;/code&gt; (class)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;data&lt;/code&gt; object becomes component state. The rule “state is private to the component unless lifted” stays unchanged.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Computed / derived values&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; or derived values inside render&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;computed&lt;/code&gt; properties are just memoized functions. In React you can achieve the same with &lt;code&gt;useMemo(() =&amp;gt; …, [deps])&lt;/code&gt; or by computing directly in the render function when cheap.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lifecycle hooks&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; (hooks) or class lifecycle methods&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;mounted&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;updated&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;beforeDestroy&lt;/code&gt; correspond to &lt;code&gt;useEffect(() =&amp;gt; { … }, [])&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useEffect(() =&amp;gt; { … })&lt;/code&gt;, and cleanup functions.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Event handling&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;v-on&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;onClick&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;onChange&lt;/code&gt; etc.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The pattern “emit an event upward” becomes calling a prop callback (&lt;code&gt;onClick={() =&amp;gt; props.onSave()}&lt;/code&gt;) or using a context/store.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Scoped CSS&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;CSS Modules / styled‑components&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;style scoped&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; isolates styles; in React you can use CSS Modules (&lt;code&gt;import styles from './Comp.module.css'&lt;/code&gt;) or CSS‑in‑JS libraries to get the same encapsulation.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Single‑file component mindset&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Separate &lt;code&gt;.jsx/.tsx&lt;/code&gt; and style files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;While Vue bundles template, script, and style, the idea of co‑locating a component’s logic, markup, and styles persists; you’ll just split them into files that are imported together.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reactivity basics&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Immutable state updates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s reactivity system automatically tracks dependencies; React requires you to treat state as immutable and call the setter to trigger a re‑render. The conceptual goal—UI reflects state—remains identical.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Testing approach&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;vue-test-utils&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;@testing-library/react&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Both encourage shallow rendering and interaction testing. Your test‑first mindset transfers without friction.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What's new
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Side‑by‑side example: a toggle button
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Vue (single‑file component)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight vue"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;template&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;@click&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"toggle"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cp"&gt;{{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ON'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'OFF'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cp"&gt;}}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/button&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/template&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toggle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/script&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;style&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;scoped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;padding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.5rem&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1rem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;&amp;lt;/style&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;React (function component with hooks)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'react'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./ToggleButton.css'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// CSS Modules or plain CSS&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ToggleButton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setIsOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;toggle&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setIsOn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;toggle&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isOn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'ON'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'OFF'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h4&gt;


What changed and why
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Template vs JSX&lt;/strong&gt; – Vue’s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;template&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; uses HTML‑like syntax with directives (&lt;code&gt;@click&lt;/code&gt;). React embeds markup directly in JavaScript via JSX, so event handlers become &lt;code&gt;onClick={handler}&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State declaration&lt;/strong&gt; – Vue’s &lt;code&gt;data&lt;/code&gt; returns an object; React uses the &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; hook which returns a tuple &lt;code&gt;[value, setter]&lt;/code&gt;. The setter must be called to trigger a re‑render.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Methods vs inline functions&lt;/strong&gt; – Vue groups handlers under &lt;code&gt;methods&lt;/code&gt;. In React you can declare functions inside the component or use &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; for memoization; the example keeps it simple.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Scoped styles&lt;/strong&gt; – Vue’s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;style scoped&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; automatically scopes selectors. React relies on CSS Modules (&lt;code&gt;ToggleButton.module.css&lt;/code&gt;) or CSS‑in‑JS to achieve isolation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;File structure&lt;/strong&gt; – Vue bundles everything in one &lt;code&gt;.vue&lt;/code&gt; file; React typically separates concerns (&lt;code&gt;.jsx&lt;/code&gt; for logic/markup, &lt;code&gt;.css&lt;/code&gt; for styles).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Other new concepts:  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hooks&lt;/strong&gt; – React’s functional paradigm revolves around hooks (&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useContext&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;). They replace Vue’s options API and give you fine‑grained control over side effects.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;JSX is JavaScript&lt;/strong&gt; – You can use any JS expression inside &lt;code&gt;{}&lt;/code&gt;; no need for Vue’s template interpolation rules.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Explicit imports&lt;/strong&gt; – Every component, hook, and utility must be imported; there is no global registration like Vue’s &lt;code&gt;app.component&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Strict immutability&lt;/strong&gt; – Updating nested state requires shallow copies (&lt;code&gt;setState(prev =&amp;gt; ({ ...prev, nested: newVal }))&lt;/code&gt;). Vue’s proxy‑based reactivity hides this, but React expects you to manage it.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Common pitfalls
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Pitfall&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Why it happens for Vue devs&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;How to avoid it&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mutating state directly&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s proxies let you write &lt;code&gt;this.foo = …&lt;/code&gt; anywhere. In React you must call the state setter.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Always use the setter (&lt;code&gt;setFoo(newVal)&lt;/code&gt;). For objects/arrays, spread‑copy before updating.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Forgetting dependency arrays in &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;watch&lt;/code&gt; runs only when watched sources change. In React an empty &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; runs once, but omitting the array makes it run on every render.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Explicitly pass &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt; for “run once” or &lt;code&gt;[dep1, dep2]&lt;/code&gt; for specific dependencies.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Assuming JSX auto‑escapes HTML&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;v-html&lt;/code&gt; is explicit; in React you must use &lt;code&gt;dangerouslySetInnerHTML&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;dangerouslySetInnerHTML&lt;/code&gt; only when necessary and sanitize input.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Using &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt; in function components&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s options API relies on &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt;. React function components have no &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Keep all logic inside the function scope or use hooks; never reference &lt;code&gt;this&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Over‑using context for simple prop drilling&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s provide/inject is often used for deep trees. React’s Context has a higher render cost if misused.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reserve Context for truly global data; otherwise lift state up and pass props.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Neglecting key props in lists&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue warns about missing &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt;. React will silently reuse DOM nodes, causing bugs.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Always provide a stable &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;key={item.id}&lt;/code&gt;) when rendering arrays.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Assuming CSS is scoped automatically&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;style scoped&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; does the work. React does not.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Use CSS Modules, styled‑components, or BEM naming to avoid collisions.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mixing default and named imports&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vue’s default export (&lt;code&gt;export default&lt;/code&gt;) is common. React components are usually default‑exported, but utilities are named.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Follow the library’s export style; &lt;code&gt;import React, { useState } from 'react'&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Your 30‑day plan
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Week 1 – Foundations
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Set up the toolchain&lt;/strong&gt; – Install Node ≥ 18, run &lt;code&gt;npx create-react-app my-app&lt;/code&gt; (or Vite with &lt;code&gt;npm create vite@latest&lt;/code&gt;). Commit the scaffold to Git.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Learn JSX basics&lt;/strong&gt; – Convert a simple Vue template (e.g., a list rendering) to JSX, paying attention to &lt;code&gt;{}&lt;/code&gt; interpolation and &lt;code&gt;className&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State with &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; – Re‑implement a Vue component that toggles visibility using &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project:&lt;/strong&gt; Build a &lt;strong&gt;Todo List&lt;/strong&gt; that adds, toggles, and deletes items. Use only functional components and &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Week 2 – Effects, Context, and Routing
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Side effects&lt;/strong&gt; – Replace a Vue &lt;code&gt;mounted&lt;/code&gt; API call with &lt;code&gt;useEffect(() =&amp;gt; { … }, [])&lt;/code&gt;. Experiment with cleanup functions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;React Router&lt;/strong&gt; – Install &lt;code&gt;react-router-dom&lt;/code&gt;, create a two‑page app (Home / About). Compare Vue Router’s &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;router-link&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;Link&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; component.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Context API&lt;/strong&gt; – Move the Todo List’s state to a Context provider and consume it in a child component. Observe re‑renders.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project:&lt;/strong&gt; Extend the Todo app with &lt;strong&gt;persistent storage&lt;/strong&gt; using &lt;code&gt;localStorage&lt;/code&gt; inside &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;. Add a settings page reachable via routing.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Week 3 – Advanced Patterns
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Custom hooks&lt;/strong&gt; – Extract the Todo logic into a &lt;code&gt;useTodos&lt;/code&gt; hook. Compare to Vue composables.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Performance&lt;/strong&gt; – Use &lt;code&gt;React.memo&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt; to prevent unnecessary renders in a list with many items.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Styling&lt;/strong&gt; – Switch from global CSS to CSS Modules or styled‑components; verify that styles are scoped.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project:&lt;/strong&gt; Create a &lt;strong&gt;Dashboard&lt;/strong&gt; with a chart (e.g., using &lt;code&gt;recharts&lt;/code&gt;) and a data table. Use custom hooks for data fetching, memoization for expensive calculations, and Context for theme toggling.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Week 4 – Ecosystem &amp;amp; Production
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State management&lt;/strong&gt; – Explore a lightweight store like &lt;strong&gt;Zustand&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;Jotai&lt;/strong&gt;; compare to Vuex/Pinia. Implement a global counter.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Testing&lt;/strong&gt; – Write unit tests with &lt;strong&gt;@testing-library/react&lt;/strong&gt; for the Dashboard components; add a few integration tests with &lt;strong&gt;Cypress&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Build &amp;amp; Deploy&lt;/strong&gt; – Run &lt;code&gt;npm run build&lt;/code&gt;, serve the static files locally, then deploy to a free static host (e.g., Netlify). Verify source maps and environment variables.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mini project:&lt;/strong&gt; Polish the Dashboard, add error boundaries, and write a short README that explains the architecture.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By the end of the month you’ll have touched every core React concept, built three progressively larger apps, and exercised the tooling that surrounds a production React codebase.  &lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


How different is the learning curve compared to moving from Vue 2 to Vue 3?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The shift to React introduces a new syntax (JSX) and a hook‑centric mental model, which is steeper than Vue’s optional Composition API but still manageable with daily practice.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Do I need to learn TypeScript for React?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not immediately; React works perfectly with plain JavaScript. However, most modern React codebases adopt TypeScript, so consider adding it after you’re comfortable with the core concepts.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Should I keep using Vue‑style single‑file components?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React does not have an exact equivalent, but you can approximate the experience with tools like &lt;strong&gt;Vite&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;MDX&lt;/strong&gt;. Most teams prefer separate &lt;code&gt;.jsx/.tsx&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;.css&lt;/code&gt; files for clarity.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Is React’s Context a replacement for Vuex/Pinia?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Context is good for low‑frequency global data (theme, locale). For complex state with actions, mutations, and devtools, a dedicated store (Zustand, Jotai, Redux) is more appropriate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Keep going
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;See what already transfers:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://switchbyskill.com/switch-check?pair=vue-to-react&amp;amp;utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=community&amp;amp;utm_campaign=blog-30-day-plan&amp;amp;utm_content=vue-developer-learning-react-what-carries-over-what-s-new-30-day" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;take the free Vue → React readiness check&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://switchbyskill.com/switch/vue-to-react" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The full Vue → React roadmap, topic by topic&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Related: &lt;a href="https://switchbyskill.com/blog/vue-developer-learning-angular-what-carries-over-what-s-new-30-day-plan" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Vue developer learning Angular: what carries over, what's new, 30-day plan&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Related: &lt;a href="https://switchbyskill.com/blog/react-developer-learning-angular-what-carries-over-what-s-new-30-day-plan" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React developer learning Angular: what carries over, what's new, 30-day plan&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published at &lt;a href="https://switchbyskill.com/blog/vue-developer-learning-react-what-carries-over-what-s-new-30-day-plan" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;switchbyskill.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We needed a new JS table library for DataHub's dataset preview: has to show a CSV of anywhere from a hundred rows to half a million.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I went looking for a comparison of table libraries and found lists: a dozen names, a feature matrix, no demos, and often a closing paragraph that just said: "it depends".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So we built a proper comparison:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://tables.datahub.io" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tables.datahub.io&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What to use
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's what we'd use.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Job&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Use&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Runner-up&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A table on a page (up to a few thousand rows)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Plain &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;table&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Observable Inputs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A big grid (up to 500,000 rows, sort and filter built in)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;AG Grid Community&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;RevoGrid&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An editable grid (edit cells, paste ranges)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Tabulator&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;RevoGrid&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you are in React and want your own markup and CSS, use TanStack Table with a virtualizer. That is what DataHub does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Flnh8xkpb5rwgt9rrouw2.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Flnh8xkpb5rwgt9rrouw2.png" alt="The nine tables over the same 1,000 rows, each with its own bundle size and line count" width="800" height="873"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What we did
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Eight libraries and a plain &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;table&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;, on the same UK Land Registry sales data at 1,000 rows, 1,000 rows by 80 columns, 50,000 and 500,000, with the same harness and cell formatter. We measured gzipped bundle size, lines written, render time, scroll FPS, sort at 500,000 rows, text selection, keyboard sort and a small edit test. We also checked 13 features per library: see the &lt;a href="https://tables.datahub.io/compare.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;compare page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What we found
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Speed doesn't pick a winner. Five grids (Glide, RevoGrid, TanStack, Tabulator, AG Grid) render 500,000 rows in 17 to 233 ms, scroll at 60 fps and sort by price in 0.1 to 0.42 s. Glide has no built-in sort, so its 0.1 s is our own code. Perspective takes 1.9 s and DataTables 8.5 s (its own FAQ says stop at about 50,000 rows).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four other things separated them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Text you can find and copy.&lt;/strong&gt; Every grid except Glide, which is canvas, puts visible cells in the DOM, so Ctrl-F finds them. Only those: a virtualized grid has the rest unmounted. Browser text selection works in the plain table, Observable, Tabulator, TanStack and DataTables. AG Grid blocks it unless you set &lt;code&gt;enableCellTextSelection&lt;/code&gt;; Perspective and RevoGrid block it. For a data site this is most of the job.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Weight.&lt;/strong&gt; From 1 kB for a plain &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;table&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; to 332 kB for AG Grid. The chart has the rest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpfolcm6rlh743vfa8whm.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fpfolcm6rlh743vfa8whm.png" alt="Library code shipped, gzipped kB, for the nine entries" width="799" height="407"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How it fails.&lt;/strong&gt; Tabulator's &lt;code&gt;height: "100%"&lt;/code&gt;, the pattern its docs give for filling a container, renders every row when the parent has no resolved height: 88 seconds at 50,000 rows against 36 ms with a sized parent, and nothing logged. AG Grid, given the same mistake, draws a visibly empty grid.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Whether it's still shipping.&lt;/strong&gt; Glide Data Grid's last stable release was February 2024 and it is React 16 to 18 only. Our first draft picked it for large data. We reversed that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The picks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A table on a page: plain &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;table&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt; 39 lines, 1 kB, 1,000 rows in about 55 ms. It prints, it copies, dark mode is CSS. But it lays out every row: 2.5 s at 50,000. For sort on click in one call, use &lt;code&gt;Inputs.table(rows)&lt;/code&gt; from Observable and keep it under about ten thousand rows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A big grid: AG Grid Community.&lt;/strong&gt; 40 lines for sort, filter and virtualization. It is 332 kB as the quick start ships it (272 kB lean). Community is MIT; grouping, pivot, range selection, clipboard and Excel export are Enterprise. Runner-up RevoGrid is the fastest DOM grid we measured at 500,000 rows (62 ms), with clipboard free, if you accept the "RevoGrid" link in the corner.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;An editable grid: Tabulator.&lt;/strong&gt; The test: double-click a price and type, then paste three lines onto a cell. Tabulator passed with options from its own spreadsheet example, all free and MIT. RevoGrid passed with no config. AG Grid Community edits a cell, but paste is Enterprise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Build your own: TanStack Table with a virtualizer.&lt;/strong&gt; Headless: it computes rows, sorting and columns and you write every element. Our demo is 150 lines. v9 is a redesign and its docs lag it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What DataHub will use
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TanStack Table with react-virtual, wrapped once as a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;DataTable&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; component in PortalJS. We already ship React, so the table costs 24 kB, not 332, and the preview is text people can Ctrl-F and copy a postcode out of. In exchange we write dataset-wide search, keyboard handling and virtualization ourselves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Method and caveats
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every figure is the median of five page loads on one M4 laptop in headless Chromium, so treat them as an order of magnitude and re-measure in your own browser. The Tabulator 88 seconds was timed by hand. Agents built and measured the demos under our direction; we chose the verdict. Here are the &lt;a href="https://github.com/datasets/tables-bakeoff/blob/main/scripts/measure-all.mjs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;script&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://github.com/datasets/tables-bakeoff/blob/main/docs/reports/measurements-2026-11-21.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;raw runs&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All nine are live, with source and the measure buttons:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://tables.datahub.io" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tables.datahub.io&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tell us what we got wrong or missed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Same treatment for line charts: &lt;a href="https://linecharts.datahub.io/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;linecharts.datahub.io&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Contains HM Land Registry data, Crown copyright and database right 2026, Open Government Licence v3.0.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Originally published on the &lt;a href="https://datahub.io/blog/javascript-table-library-bakeoff" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;DataHub blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


JavaScript &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; Is Coming to V8: What the TC39 Proposal Means for Cross-Realm Error Checking
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This article was written with the assistance of AI, under human supervision and review.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; static method checks whether a value is an &lt;code&gt;Error&lt;/code&gt; instance using a branded check mechanism that works across realms, addressing silent failures in &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; when errors cross iframe, worker, or Node.js vm module boundaries. The TC39 proposal reached Stage 4 and ships in Chrome 134+, Firefox 138+, and Node.js 23.0+.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fq48f3o6hrmjt10hb35u0.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fq48f3o6hrmjt10hb35u0.png" alt="Problem diagram showing instanceof Error failing with cross-realm error" width="800" height="135"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Problem diagram showing instanceof Error failing with cross-realm error&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers face two distinct failure modes with &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt;. An error thrown in an iframe and caught in the parent window returns false because each realm has its own &lt;code&gt;Error&lt;/code&gt; constructor. The opposite problem hits when a plain object with &lt;code&gt;Error.prototype&lt;/code&gt; in its chain passes the &lt;code&gt;instanceof&lt;/code&gt; test despite lacking the internal error state. The &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; method from &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Error/isError" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN's official documentation&lt;/a&gt; uses the same branded check strategy as &lt;code&gt;Array.isArray()&lt;/code&gt;, inspecting a private field initialized by the &lt;code&gt;Error()&lt;/code&gt; constructor rather than walking the prototype chain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsypznpd3vi9z94lunfbb.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsypznpd3vi9z94lunfbb.png" alt="Solution diagram showing Error.isError() correctly identifying cross-realm error" width="800" height="135"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Solution diagram showing Error.isError() correctly identifying cross-realm error&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Key Takeaways
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; performs a branded check for a private field, avoiding both false positives from prototype manipulation and false negatives from cross-realm errors.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The method ships in Chrome 134+, Firefox 138+, and Node.js 23.0+, with no polyfill needed for environments where the check matters most.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Global error handlers, test frameworks, and SSR runtimes benefit immediately because they aggregate errors from multiple execution contexts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;DOMException&lt;/code&gt; instances return true from &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; despite not inheriting from &lt;code&gt;Error&lt;/code&gt;, matching their branded behavior across the platform.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Migration from &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; applies only to code paths that receive errors from iframes, workers, or vm modules; local error handling remains unchanged.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why instanceof Error Silently Fails in Modern JavaScript
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; operator checks whether &lt;code&gt;Error.prototype&lt;/code&gt; appears in the value's prototype chain. That check assumes the &lt;code&gt;Error&lt;/code&gt; constructor in the current realm is the same constructor that created the error. When an error crosses a realm boundary, the assumption breaks. An error created in an iframe has &lt;code&gt;iframeError.prototype&lt;/code&gt; as its prototype, not the parent window's &lt;code&gt;Error.prototype&lt;/code&gt;. The &lt;code&gt;instanceof&lt;/code&gt; check in the parent window returns false even though the value is a genuine error with a stack trace and message.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The second failure mode is the inverse problem. Developers can create a plain object, set its prototype to &lt;code&gt;Error.prototype&lt;/code&gt;, and pass the &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; check without the object ever being constructed by the &lt;code&gt;Error()&lt;/code&gt; constructor. That object lacks the internal slots required for error handling, so operations like stack trace formatting fail. The pattern appears in test mocks and legacy codebases that manually wire up prototypes.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// False negative: real error from iframe&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;iframe&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'iframe'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;appendChild&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;iframe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;iframeError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;iframe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;contentWindow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Cross-realm error'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;iframeError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// false&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;iframeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// true&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// False positive: prototype manipulation&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fakeError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prototype&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fakeError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// true&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fakeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// false&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Execution_model" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN execution model documentation&lt;/a&gt; defines realms as the boundaries where these checks fail. Each realm owns a list of intrinsic objects including &lt;code&gt;Array&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Error&lt;/code&gt;, and their prototypes. When code in one realm receives a value from another, the prototype identity check in &lt;code&gt;instanceof&lt;/code&gt; compares against the wrong intrinsic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fslmx8qdld7gjfm26tdos.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fslmx8qdld7gjfm26tdos.jpg" alt="JavaScript realms showing separate Error constructors in iframe and parent window" width="467" height="350"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;JavaScript realms showing separate Error constructors in iframe and parent window&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The TC39 proposal addresses both failure modes by checking for a private field that only the &lt;code&gt;Error()&lt;/code&gt; constructor can initialize. That field exists regardless of realm, so an error from an iframe has the field even though its prototype chain points to a different &lt;code&gt;Error.prototype&lt;/code&gt;. The branded check rejects fake errors because they lack the field, even if their prototype chain includes &lt;code&gt;Error.prototype&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Understanding Realms: iframes, Workers, and vm Modules
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A realm consists of a global object, a set of intrinsic objects, and the environment records for globally declared variables. Each &lt;code&gt;iframe&lt;/code&gt; element creates a new realm. Web Workers and Service Workers execute in separate realms. Node.js provides the &lt;code&gt;vm&lt;/code&gt; module to create isolated realms for running untrusted code. The &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Execution_model" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN realm documentation&lt;/a&gt; specifies that each realm has its own &lt;code&gt;globalThis&lt;/code&gt; and its own copies of built-in constructors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F4joi7g25ic3q4gub0l4r.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F4joi7g25ic3q4gub0l4r.png" alt="Diagram showing realm hierarchy with separate Error constructors" width="800" height="403"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Diagram showing realm hierarchy with separate Error constructors&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The practical consequence is that &lt;code&gt;iframe.contentWindow.Error !== window.Error&lt;/code&gt;. When an iframe throws an error and the parent catches it via &lt;code&gt;postMessage&lt;/code&gt; or by calling a function that throws, the error's constructor is the iframe's &lt;code&gt;Error&lt;/code&gt;, not the parent's. The &lt;code&gt;instanceof&lt;/code&gt; operator in the parent compares against &lt;code&gt;window.Error.prototype&lt;/code&gt;, finds no match, and returns false.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Node.js vm module creates a separate realm&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;vm&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'vm'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sandbox&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;vm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sandbox&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;vm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;runInContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`
try {
throw new Error('Sandbox error');
} catch (e) {
result = e;
}
`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sandbox&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// false&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sandbox&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// true&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Workers communicate through &lt;code&gt;postMessage&lt;/code&gt;, which uses structured cloning. Structured cloning serializes errors by preserving their message, stack, and cause, then reconstructs them in the receiving realm. The reconstructed error has the receiving realm's &lt;code&gt;Error.prototype&lt;/code&gt;, so &lt;code&gt;instanceof&lt;/code&gt; succeeds in the receiver but fails if the original error object is passed through a shared reference mechanism like &lt;code&gt;SharedArrayBuffer&lt;/code&gt; metadata.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The vm module in Node.js exposes the realm boundary explicitly. Code executed in a vm context runs in a separate realm with its own global object and intrinsics. Errors thrown in that context belong to the context's &lt;code&gt;Error&lt;/code&gt; constructor, not the main realm's. The &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; method works in both directions: it identifies errors from vm contexts when called in the main realm, and it identifies main-realm errors when called inside a vm context.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How Error.isError() Works Under the Hood
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; method performs a branded check by looking for a private field that the &lt;code&gt;Error()&lt;/code&gt; constructor adds to every error instance. That private field is not accessible to JavaScript code and cannot be forged. The &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Error/isError" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN Error.isError documentation&lt;/a&gt; states that the mechanism is the same as &lt;code&gt;Array.isArray()&lt;/code&gt;, which checks for an internal slot rather than walking the prototype chain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fqgw9yyefglv0cql6ks38.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fqgw9yyefglv0cql6ks38.png" alt="Flowchart showing Error.isError() branded check process" width="800" height="1031"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Flowchart showing Error.isError() branded check process&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The private field is initialized in the &lt;code&gt;Error()&lt;/code&gt; constructor before any user code runs. Subclasses like &lt;code&gt;TypeError&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;RangeError&lt;/code&gt; inherit the same initialization, so &lt;code&gt;Error.isError(new TypeError())&lt;/code&gt; returns true. The check ignores the prototype chain entirely. An object can have &lt;code&gt;Error.prototype&lt;/code&gt; as its prototype but lack the private field if it was created with &lt;code&gt;Object.create(Error.prototype)&lt;/code&gt; instead of &lt;code&gt;new Error()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The method returns true for &lt;code&gt;DOMException&lt;/code&gt; instances even though &lt;code&gt;DOMException&lt;/code&gt; does not inherit from &lt;code&gt;Error&lt;/code&gt; in the prototype chain. The &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Error/isError" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN documentation&lt;/a&gt; explains that &lt;code&gt;DOMException&lt;/code&gt; behaves like &lt;code&gt;Error&lt;/code&gt; for all branded checking purposes, meaning the &lt;code&gt;DOMException()&lt;/code&gt; constructor initializes the same private field. This keeps error-handling code consistent across platform errors and JavaScript errors.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// All Error subclasses return true&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// true&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;TypeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// true&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RangeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// true&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// DOMException returns true despite not inheriting from Error&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;DOMException&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// true&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Fake error returns false&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fake&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;__proto__&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prototype&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'fake'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fake&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// false&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The branded check runs in constant time regardless of the prototype chain length. The &lt;code&gt;instanceof&lt;/code&gt; operator must walk the entire prototype chain, checking each step against the target constructor's prototype. For deeply nested class hierarchies, &lt;code&gt;instanceof&lt;/code&gt; becomes a linear-time operation. The branded check in &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; accesses a single internal slot and returns, making it faster than &lt;code&gt;instanceof&lt;/code&gt; in most cases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The method accepts any value as its argument and returns false for non-objects without throwing. Passing &lt;code&gt;undefined&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt;, a number, or a string to &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; returns false. The same inputs cause &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; to throw a TypeError when the right operand is not callable, though &lt;code&gt;instanceof&lt;/code&gt; accepts primitives on the left side and returns false.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmldyw8c6cyadoeyw3yfk.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmldyw8c6cyadoeyw3yfk.jpg" alt="Diagram showing Error.isError() internal mechanism with private field check" width="525" height="350"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Diagram showing Error.isError() internal mechanism with private field check&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Comparing Error Detection Methods: instanceof vs Object.prototype.toString vs Error.isError()
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers have used three patterns to check for errors: &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Object.prototype.toString.call(value) === '[object Error]'&lt;/code&gt;, and now &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt;. Each has different failure modes and performance characteristics. The table shows when each method succeeds or fails.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Method&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Real error (same realm)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Real error (cross-realm)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Fake error (prototype only)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;DOMException&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Performance&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✓&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✗&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✓ (false positive)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✗&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;O(n) chain walk&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;Object.prototype.toString&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✓&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✓&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✗ with Symbol.toStringTag&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✓&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;O(1) but spoofable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✓&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✓&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✗&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;✓&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;O(1) branded check&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fwo8w5fj4u2iu3i1uo6jj.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fwo8w5fj4u2iu3i1uo6jj.png" alt="Comparison flowchart showing three error detection methods" width="800" height="932"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Comparison flowchart showing three error detection methods&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; check walks the prototype chain until it finds &lt;code&gt;Error.prototype&lt;/code&gt; or reaches null. That walk is O(n) where n is the chain length. For most errors n is small, but custom error subclasses with deep inheritance trees pay the cost. The check fails for cross-realm errors because it looks for the wrong &lt;code&gt;Error.prototype&lt;/code&gt;, and it succeeds for fake errors because it only checks the chain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;Object.prototype.toString.call(value)&lt;/code&gt; pattern returns &lt;code&gt;'[object Error]'&lt;/code&gt; for genuine errors. That check worked reliably before ES2015 introduced &lt;code&gt;Symbol.toStringTag&lt;/code&gt;. Now any object can override its string tag by defining &lt;code&gt;Symbol.toStringTag&lt;/code&gt;, making the check spoofable. A plain object with &lt;code&gt;{ [Symbol.toStringTag]: 'Error' }&lt;/code&gt; returns &lt;code&gt;'[object Error]'&lt;/code&gt; from &lt;code&gt;toString&lt;/code&gt; even though it is not an error.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// instanceof fails cross-realm&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;iframe&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;createElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'iframe'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;appendChild&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;iframe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;crossRealmError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;iframe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;contentWindow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'test'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;crossRealmError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// false&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;crossRealmError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// true&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// toString is spoofable&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;spoofed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Symbol&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;toStringTag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Error'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'fake'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prototype&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;toString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;call&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;spoofed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// '[object Error]'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;spoofed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// false&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Error.isError() rejects fake errors&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fake&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;create&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prototype&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fake&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// true (false positive)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fake&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// false&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; method provides the strongest guarantee. It returns true only for values created by the &lt;code&gt;Error()&lt;/code&gt; constructor or its subclasses, regardless of realm. The branded check cannot be spoofed because the private field is not accessible from JavaScript. The method runs in constant time and allocates no memory, making it the fastest option.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The choice depends on the deployment environment. Local error handling in a single realm can continue using &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; because the cross-realm case does not arise. Code that aggregates errors from multiple sources must use &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; to avoid silent failures. Test frameworks, logging libraries, and global error handlers fall into the second category.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Real-World Code Examples: Global Error Handlers and Cross-Realm Error Detection
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Global error handlers aggregate errors from the entire application, including iframes, workers, and dynamically loaded modules. The handlers must distinguish errors from non-error exceptions because JavaScript allows throwing any value. The pattern before &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; was to check &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; and fall back to wrapping non-error values.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Before Error.isError(): unreliable global error handler&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'error'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// instanceof fails for cross-realm errors&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Caught error:'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;stack&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// This branch catches cross-realm errors incorrectly&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Caught non-error:'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// After Error.isError(): reliable global error handler&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'error'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Caught error:'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;stack&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Caught non-error:'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Test frameworks encounter the cross-realm case when running tests in iframes or workers. The framework's assertion library might reject errors from the wrong realm if it uses &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; to validate exception types. Switching to &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; fixes the false negative without changing the test API.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Test framework error normalization&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;normalizeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Before: instanceof Error fails for cross-realm errors&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// After: Error.isError() handles cross-realm correctly&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;normalizeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Usage in test runner&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;runTestInIframe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;testFn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;normalized&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;normalizeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Test failed: ${normalized.message}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;normalized&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;stack&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Server-side rendering frameworks that run user code in isolated contexts benefit from &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; when aggregating errors across contexts. Node.js applications using the vm module to sandbox user code need the cross-realm check to log errors correctly. The pattern normalizes errors before passing them to logging infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://jsmanifest.com/iterator-helpers-baseline-2026" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;related iterator helpers post&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://jsmanifest.com/float16array-baseline-2026-ml-graphics" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Float16Array introduction&lt;/a&gt; show similar patterns where new built-in methods solve long-standing cross-realm issues. The &lt;a href="https://jsmanifest.com/tc39-type-annotations-proposal-2026" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TC39 type annotations proposal&lt;/a&gt; adds static types without changing runtime behavior, complementing the runtime safety that &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; provides.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Browser and Runtime Support: Chrome 134+, Firefox 138+, and Node.js
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; method ships in Chrome 134 and later, Firefox 138 and later, and Node.js 23.0 and later. Safari has not shipped the feature as of late 2026. The &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Error/isError" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN browser compatibility data&lt;/a&gt; tracks implementation status across engines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxjxltld5zwfscz98ug8c.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxjxltld5zwfscz98ug8c.png" alt="Browser support timeline for Error.isError()" width="800" height="135"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Browser support timeline for Error.isError()&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can check for support with a feature test before calling the method. The test avoids runtime errors in older environments and provides a fallback path. The fallback should use a conservative check that accepts more values as errors rather than rejecting genuine errors.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Feature detection&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hasErrorIsError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;typeof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'function'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;is&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hasErrorIsError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Fallback: conservative check&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Accepts cross-realm errors and some false positives&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; 
&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;typeof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'object'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'message'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'stack'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Polyfilling &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; is not possible because the private field is not accessible from JavaScript. The branded check requires engine support. Libraries that need cross-realm error detection before engine support arrives must use heuristics that check for error-like properties. Those heuristics accept false positives to avoid false negatives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Node.js 23.0 ships the method in both the main thread and worker threads. The vm module creates contexts where &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; is available on the sandbox's &lt;code&gt;Error&lt;/code&gt; constructor. Errors created in a vm context can be checked with &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; from the main thread, and errors from the main thread can be checked inside the vm context.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The lack of Safari support means production code targeting iOS must include the feature test and fallback. Web applications can serve the feature-detected code path to Chrome and Firefox users while serving the conservative fallback to Safari users. The performance difference between the two paths is negligible for error handling, which is not a hot path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Migration Strategy: When to Replace instanceof Error in Your Codebase
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Replacing &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; is not a blanket recommendation. The migration applies only to code paths that receive errors from multiple realms. Local error handling within a single realm sees no benefit from the change. The decision tree starts with identifying which code paths aggregate errors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6t575vqjvvyad1fqjd0l.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6t575vqjvvyad1fqjd0l.png" alt="Decision flowchart for migrating from instanceof to Error.isError()" width="799" height="235"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Decision flowchart for migrating from instanceof to Error.isError()&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Global error handlers must migrate because they receive errors from all origins including iframes and workers. Test frameworks that execute tests in isolated contexts must migrate to avoid false negatives when validating thrown errors. Logging libraries that normalize caught exceptions before sending them to backend services should migrate to handle cross-realm errors correctly.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Identify migration targets by scanning for these patterns&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 1. Global error handlers&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'error'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;handler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'unhandledrejection'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;handler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 2. Test framework assertion helpers&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toThrow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;throws&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 3. Cross-realm communication&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;iframe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;contentWindow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;someFunction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;worker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;postMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'task'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// 4. Node.js vm module usage&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;vm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;runInContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Code that only catches errors from local try-catch blocks does not need migration. Exception handling in pure functions, class methods, and async functions remains unchanged. The &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; check works correctly when the error originates in the same realm.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The migration steps are: add a feature detection check, replace &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; with the feature-detected &lt;code&gt;isError()&lt;/code&gt; helper, and add test cases for cross-realm errors. The test cases should create an iframe or worker, throw an error from it, and verify the detection succeeds.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Migration example: before&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Logger&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;logError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;logValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Migration example: after&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Logger&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;is&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;typeof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'function'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;logError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kc"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;logValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The migration is non-breaking because &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; returns true for all values where &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; returns true in the same realm, plus it fixes the false negatives. Code that relied on the false positives from prototype manipulation breaks, but that code was already brittle. The false positives occur when developers intentionally create fake error objects, which is a rare pattern outside of tests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does Error.isError() work with custom error classes?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Custom error classes that extend &lt;code&gt;Error&lt;/code&gt; receive the same private field during construction, so &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; returns true for instances of custom error classes regardless of which realm created them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Can I polyfill Error.isError() in older browsers?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. The branded check requires access to a private internal field that JavaScript cannot access. The best alternative is a feature-detected fallback that uses &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; as a conservative approximation, accepting that it will fail for cross-realm errors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Does Error.isError() throw when passed undefined or null?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. The method returns false for all non-object values including undefined, null, numbers, strings, and symbols. The behavior matches &lt;code&gt;Array.isArray()&lt;/code&gt;, which also returns false for primitives without throwing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


What happens when Error.isError() receives a Promise rejection value?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Promises can reject with any value, not just errors. When a Promise rejects with an error, &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; on the rejection value returns true. When a Promise rejects with a non-error value like a string, the method returns false.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Why does Error.isError() return true for DOMException?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;DOMException()&lt;/code&gt; constructor initializes the same private field as the &lt;code&gt;Error()&lt;/code&gt; constructor, allowing platform errors and JavaScript errors to be detected with the same method. This keeps error-handling code consistent across error sources.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Error.isError() Is Now Part of ECMAScript 2026
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;Error.isError()&lt;/code&gt; method reached Stage 4 in the TC39 process and is part of ECMAScript 2026. The branded check mechanism fixes both false negatives from cross-realm errors and false positives from prototype manipulation. Developers working with iframes, workers, or Node.js vm modules should migrate global error handlers and test frameworks to use the new method. Local error handling in single-realm code paths can continue using &lt;code&gt;instanceof Error&lt;/code&gt; without issues. The feature ships in Chrome 134+, Firefox 138+, and Node.js 23.0+, with Safari support pending.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Error/isError" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Error.isError() - JavaScript | MDN&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At 3:07 a.m. the on-call page was not a model timeout. It was a deck editor that had stopped painting. An agent had scaffolded a twelve-slide internal review, someone resized a callout, and the main thread spent the rest of the drag in layout. By the time focus fell out of the theme dialog, screen-reader users were reading canvas nodes they could not see. We had adopted chuspeeism/dashi-ppt-skill as the generation layer, and the first production path showed that generation can be slow. The editor cannot be.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The integration splits into three stages with a hard boundary between them. The skill selects a visual theme and scaffolds slide markup. A React shell owns live editing. A pure export function snapshots the committed DOM into HTML, PDF, or PPTX. That separation is what makes the skill usable once a human starts dragging and tabbing. We do not own the skill. We own the shell that refuses to animate layout and the exporter that trusts only committed sizes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first integration reproduced two failures slide tutorials skip. Resizing a callout wrote &lt;code&gt;style.width&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;style.height&lt;/code&gt; from a pointer-move handler. Each write forced layout, the read in the same frame observed the new box, and frames dropped until pointerup. In parallel, the theme picker was marked &lt;code&gt;role="dialog"&lt;/code&gt; with no focus trap. Tab left the overlay, the virtual cursor announced hidden canvas content, and keyboard users lost their place. Both bugs are layout and focus model violations, not missing libraries. Recovery is operational, not a retry of the model: drop the in-flight drag, ignore uncommitted transforms, and restore focus to the control that opened the dialog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The dialog below is the editor surface we mount over any slide the skill emits. It stays inside Tailwind utilities and the platform focus order. Escape closes, Tab cycles only the dialog's controls, and &lt;code&gt;aria-modal&lt;/code&gt; plus an explicit label keep the accessibility tree honest. An empty focusable set does not get a synthetic trap; Escape still closes.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
